Student Affairs Office – Senior Student Counsellor
(Ref: SSC (SAO) 2026-09-18)
Responsibilities
lead and coordinate a professional team providing counselling and SEN support services;
provide psychological counselling services to undergraduate and postgraduate students, including the assessment and management of complex cases;
serve as a key liaison with faculty members, academic departments, administrative units and external stakeholders in coordinating support arrangements and interventions for students with complex support needs;
provide professional consultation and psychological support for students with SEN presenting with psychological, behavioural or adjustment needs;
design and deliver programmes, training and workshops to promote mental wellness, resilience, inclusion and personal development; and
contribute to campus-wide initiatives to foster a supportive, inclusive and mentally healthy campus environment.
Applicants Should
possess a recognised master’s degree in Counselling, Counselling Psychology, Clinical Psychology, Mental Health, Social Work or a related discipline, with formal clinical training and supervised practice;
possess a professional qualification and/or membership of a recognised counselling, psychology or mental health professional body in Hong Kong or overseas, and adhere to the relevant professional and ethical standards;
have a minimum of 8 years of relevant post-qualification experience in counselling, preferably gained in tertiary education, youth services, mental health or related settings;
have a good command of written and spoken English and Chinese (Cantonese and Putonghua); and
possess strong communication and interpersonal skills, and be able to engage effectively with both local and non-local students.
Appointee may be required to work outside normal office hours. E xperience in delivering training, workshops or wellbeing programmes will have an advantage, particularly in areas of mental health awareness and early intervention. E xperience in supporting students with mental health concerns, special educational needs (SEN) or other complex support needs will also be an advantage.
