ef8691bfa7222b3e4b4b730a.description:
Counsellor (Fractional)
[Ref. 2026/NACAD037]
UOW College Hong Kong (“UOWCHK”) is an approved post-secondary college registered under the Post Secondary Colleges Ordinance (Cap 320). Being part of the University of Wollongong (UOW) in Australia, our students’ learning experiences are enriched through visits by UOW academics or exchange programmes with UOW and its network universities in Malaysia, Dubai and India.
Our wide range of academic programmes include bachelor’s degrees, UOW top-up degrees, sub-degrees, as well as diploma and Employees Retraining Board courses.
With about 1,700 students, the College attaches high importance to student learning experience. Overseas study tours, mentorship programmes, and internships are organized to prepare our students for the real world.
Duties
The appointee will be required to:
Provide individual and group counselling services to students;
Support students with Special Educational Needs (SEN) by assessing their needs and coordinating appropriate support services to facilitate their learning and development;
Work with academic and administrative units to identify and support students experiencing academic, personal, emotional, or behavioural difficulties;
Collaborate with Faculties in the planning and implementation of student development and wellbeing programmes that support students’ adjustment to College life and preparation for further study and employment;
Assist in handling student crisis cases and support the co-ordination of appropriate interventions and follow-up actions with academic staff, families, and external service providers;
Contribute to the planning, implementation, and evaluation of student support initiatives and new projects;
Maintain appropriate records and prepare reports related to counselling and student support services; and
Perform any other duties as assigned by supervisors.
Requirements
Applicants should possess:
a Bachelor's degree in Social Work, Counselling, Psychology, or a related discipline;
at least three years of post-qualification experience in personal, career, or student counselling for young people, preferably gained in a tertiary or educational setting;
sound knowledge of student development, mental health, and wellbeing issues affecting young adults;
good digital literacy, including proficiency in AI-enabled tools and standard computer applications;
experience in supporting students with Special Educational Needs (SEN), learning difficulties, or mental health concerns, which will be an advantage;
strong organisational and case management skills, with the ability to handle multiple priorities effectively in a dynamic environment; and
the ability to work independently as well as collaboratively with academic and administrative teams.
Salary and Conditions of Service
Remuneration package will be highly competitive, commensurate with qualifications and experience. The successful candidate will be appointed on a fractional basis of three days per week under an initial six-month contract. Subject to operational requirements and satisfactory performance, it is anticipated that the appointment may be extended beyond the initial contract period.
Information and Application
Please submit a Curriculum Vitae and a completed Application Form to UOWCHK, c/o Human Resources Unit, UOW College Hong Kong, 1/F, 18 Che Kung Miu Road, Tai Wai, N.T., Hong Kong or by Email: uowchk-hr@uow.edu.au . The Application Form is available for download under “Job Applications” on our website.
Please note that applications submitted without a complete Application Form will not be processed. The closing date for applications is 15 October 2026 . Please quote the reference number in the application. Applications will receive full consideration and only shortlisted applicants will be contacted.
UOWCHK is an equal opportunity employer and we are committed to the principles of diversity, inclusiveness and equality. Personal data provided by applicants will be used strictly in accordance with UOWCHK’s personal data policy and for recruitment and other employment-related purposes.

